    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 232005\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n105 PM MST Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Below normal temperatures will continue through the weekend before \nreadings warm back closer to the seasonal average for the bulk of \nnext week.\n\n- Aside from a few showers over the mountains of Arizona Saturday, \ndry and tranquil weather conditions will grace the region through \nthe middle of next week.\n\n- A return to inclement weather with increased rainfall chances is \npossible towards the end of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Sunday/... \nEarly afternoon WV imagery shows elongated, positively tilted \ntroughing over the western Conus with a prominent vorticity center \nlifting north across the Great Basin, while trailing shortwave \nenergy starts to fill into the height weakness over the Southwest. \nBehind the initial Great Basin wave, a cold front has cleared \neastward into New Mexico with a deep layer of drier air now \nentrenched over the forecast area. Forecast confidence is excellent \nthat a vorticity center currently descending south along the Sierra \nrange will carve into the trough base over the lower Colorado River \nvalley in the next 24 hours before slowly lifting into the four \ncorners during the weekend. This evolution will ensure H5 heights \nhover in a 565-572dm range through early next week yielding high \ntemperatures 4F-8F below the daily normals and very comfortable \novernight lows. While better quality moisture has already been \nscoured from the region leaving mixing ratios only 5-6 g/kg, the \nprogression of the midlevel cold core over the mountains of central \nArizona Saturday afternoon may squeeze out a few showers, albeit \nrather inconsequential while leaving lower elevations enjoying a \ndelightful autumn weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Monday through Friday/... \nBenign weather conditions will continue well into next week with the \nlingering trough finally moving out of the region and being replaced \nby weak ridging to zonal westerly flow. This pattern shift will \nmaintain mostly clear skies and dry conditions, while allowing \ntemperatures to gradually warm back into a near normal range through \nat least the first half of next week. Expect the bulk of the lower \ndeserts to have daily highs in the lower to middle 80s and lows from \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s during the first half of next week. Model \nuncertainty grows later next week as there are hints at some \nmoisture return into the region from the southeast, then a potential \natmospheric river approaching by next weekend. Either, or both of \nthese moisture sources may eventually help to bring a return to rain \nchances at some point before the end of the month, but it is too \nearly to have a good idea on timing and/or placement for any weather \nfeature this far in advance. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo weather concerns are expected through the TAF period. Westerly to \nnorthwesterly winds will persist through the afternoon and evening \nhours with periods of VRB intermixed. Just past mid-night tonight \nwinds will shift back out of the east, and look to resume typical \ndiurnal tendencies tomorrow. Wind speeds will be aob 07 kts. Skies \nwill be mostly clear with FEW mid level cloud decks developing later \nin the period. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns are anticipated through the for TAF \nperiod. Winds will favor a westerly component at KIPL while KBLH \nwill remain out of the NW. Speeds should remain light around 5-7 kts \nor lower. FEW passing mid level clouds during the overnight hours at \nKIPL, otherwise mostly clear skies at both terminals. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier air has spread through the area since yesterday morning \nresulting in clearing skies and an end to the rain chances. The \nweather system will still linger across the region over the next \ncouple of days bringing light shower chances to mountain areas \nSaturday, but no wetting rainfall is expected. Minimum humidity \nlevels will fall into a 25-50% range following good to excellent \novernight recovery. Light winds are forecast for today and for much \nof the area on Saturday with only some breeziness possible early \nSaturday across the western districts. Overall quiet weather \nconditions are forecast Sunday into next week with fairly stable \nhumidities and temperatures rebounding back into the normal range by \nTuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18/Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
